<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zolta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Zoltán, Grand Prince of the Hungarians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zolta, or Zoltán, was a Hungarian grand prince of the 10th century, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> he married the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> and inherited his lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. Hungarian chronicles name him as Árpád's successor, but the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> does not mention him, and his reign and dates are uncertain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zolta.jpg" alt="Zolta" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sculptor: ? photo: own work · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solt_in_Solt.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zoltán, also written Zolta, Zsolt, Solt or Zaltas, is the third Grand Prince of the Hungarians in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, a late twelfth-century chronicle by the writer known as Anonymus, which is the main source for his life. According to Anonymus he was the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> and was born during his father's second campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a>, one of the local rulers who appear only in that chronicle among the opponents of the Hungarians during the conquest of the Carpathian Basin.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> was forced to surrender and give his daughter in marriage to Zoltán, in 904 or 905. When he died, Zoltán inherited his father-in-law's duchy east of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>. His name seems to derive from the Arabian title sultan through Turkic mediation, though scholars have not unanimously accepted this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Anonymus says that Zoltán, still a minor, succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>, who died around 907, and describes him as a lisping, pale, warlike duke, merciful to his subjects but covetous of power. When he was thirteen, the leading men appointed rectors beneath the prince to settle lawsuits by customary law. In this account he later abdicated in favour of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksony">Taksony</a> and died in the third year of his son's reign.</p><p>Modern historians reject most of these details. Gyula Kristó places his birth around 880 rather than 903, and Alexandru Madgearu writes that either he was born many years earlier or his marriage came years after 904. Pál Engel calls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menumorut">Menumorut</a> an imaginary figure invented to describe the heroic wars of the conquerors, while Charles R. Bowlus takes him for a Moravian ruler whose daughter's marriage symbolised the end of Great Moravia, and Tudor Sălăgean considers him a real ruler of a duchy inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and other peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>That Zoltán ever ruled the federation of Hungarian tribes is also challenged. Sándor L. Tóth observes that as the youngest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>'s four sons he could hardly precede his elder brothers in the succession, and Kristó notes that other Hungarian chroniclers do not mention his rule, suggesting that Anonymus inserted him into the incompletely preserved list of grand princes because all Hungarian monarchs of the House of Árpád descended from him. There is consensus that, even if he never ascended the throne, all rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from that house after around 955 descended from him through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksony">Taksony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zoltán of Hungary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hungarian forces besieged Bihar, and he agreed to marry his daughter to Árpád's son <b>Zoltán</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-menumorut">Duchy of Menumorut</a></li></ul></section>
